.TH KARICTL 8 "2026-11-14" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karictl.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karictl \- Remote Control and Management Utility for KariDNS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karictl [\-c config_path | \-f config_path] [\-s socket_path] [\-t seconds] <command> [arguments...]
karictl \-v | \-\-version
karictl \-h | \-\-help
karictl tsig\-keygen [keyname]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkarictl\fR is the management utility for the \fBkaridns(8)\fR authoritative
DNS server daemon. It communicates with the daemon over a local UNIX domain
socket (default \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR) and authenticates each
connection with an HMAC challenge-response handshake: the server sends a
64-character challenge, and \fBkarictl\fR answers with the HMAC of the challenge
keyed with the shared secret. The HMAC algorithm is \fBcontrol\-channel {
algorithm }\fR on the server and \fBalgorithm\fR in \fBkarictl.conf\fR (both
default to \fBhmac\-sha256\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB\-c config_path\fR, \fB\-f config_path\fR
Path to the \fBkarictl\fR configuration file (default:
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karictl.conf\fR; if that file does not exist and
\fB/usr/local/etc/karictl.conf\fR does, the latter is used).
.TP 8
\fB\-s socket_path\fR
UNIX domain socket to connect to. Takes precedence over the \fBsocket\fR setting
of the configuration file (default: \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-t seconds\fR
How long to wait for each part of the server's reply: the challenge, and then
each read of the answer (1\[u2013]86400, default 60, as \fBrndc \-t\fR). When
the server sends nothing for that long, \fBkarictl\fR prints \fBno reply from
the server within N s\fR and fails. A full \fBreload\fR of the zone files
answers when the reload is done, so a server with very many zone files may need
a larger value; database zones answer at once (\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the
progress).
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display version information and exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the command list with the \fBreload <zone> [view] [force]\fR syntax and
examples, and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that take a zone name accept an optional view name as the next
argument. The view name is required when the zone exists in more than one view;
otherwise the command fails with \fBERROR zone exists in multiple views; specify
view\fR.
.TP 8
\fBstatus\fR
Query and display runtime statistics from the running \fBkaridns\fR daemon:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
Version, host name, OS, architecture and kernel release and CPU count of the
running server (taken when it started), and its worker thread count
.IP \(bu 2
Server boot time, time of the last successful (re)configuration (both to the
second) and the configuration file path
.PP
\fBkarictl\fR and \fBkaridns\fR must be the same version: the reply is a binary
structure, and a reply of another size is shown as is and exits with status 3.
.IP \(bu 2
Number of zones, \fBdebug level: N\fR (the debug level in force: \fBkaridns
\-d\fR, \fBtrace\fR, \fBnotrace\fR; where \fBrndc status\fR prints it) and
number of running AXFR/IXFR transfers
.IP \(bu 2
Whether query and response logging are enabled
.IP \(bu 2
Current and high-water TCP connection counts
.IP \(bu 2
Size of the zone versions and IXFR deltas (D1a): the bytes of the published
versions of the zones (the version, its image and its glue block) and of the
IXFR deltas the zones keep (the records of the difference and the data in them
as it is used, with the overhead of each block: a difference of one SOA counts
about 1.8 KB; 0.7), for the zones that come from the database and for the
others, as \fBresident\fR (what the zones serve now) and \fBretired\fR (versions
that a newer one replaced and that are not freed yet, because a reader or a
transfer may still hold them). Since 0.7 the bytes of the zone entry itself (the
entry, the lock it makes, a database zone's settings block, about 0.85 KB) are
in it, counted with the zone's first version, and the images and glue blocks are
counted at the size the allocator really holds. The directory, the response
cache and the park tables are not in it. An IXFR delta that left the history
stays counted as resident until the last transfer that reads it has released
it. A database zone that \fBmemory\-limit\fR takes out of memory gives up its
IXFR deltas at once (they are not served while the zone is out of memory, and
the zone comes back without them), so evicting and fetching a zone again does
not add to the resident bytes. Read only: nothing limits them yet
.IP \(bu 2
The bytes the zones keep for \fBrollback\fR (the previous versions, see
\fBrollback\fR): \fBzone versions kept for rollback: database zones N bytes,
other zones N bytes\fR. They are not served; they are freed when the
\fBrollback\-window\fR is over, or earlier when the database \fBmemory\-limit\fR
needs the room
.IP \(bu 2
What waits to be freed (0.7): \fBretired zone snapshots waiting: N (oldest N
ms)\fR (zone-db snapshots that a reload or a change replaced and that a reader
still holds: a dynamic update, a control command, a zone transfer that waits for
a database zone to be read (\fBtransfer\-fetch\-timeout\fR), or a thread inside
a read section; they are freed in order, so one held snapshot keeps the later
ones too. A transfer that is being sent holds none, 0.7 R11), \fBretired zone
versions waiting: N\fR (zone versions and objects on the retire stack, not freed
yet: a reader that stays in its read section keeps every version replaced after
it entered (a reload, an UPDATE, a database batch never wait for it: the count
rises and falls when it leaves), a reader holds them, or the version is the one
a running outbound transfer sends: it is freed when that transfer ends) and
\fBreclaim warnings (snapshot held over 60 s): N\fR. Nothing limits these and no
reader is cut off; when the oldest snapshot has waited more than 60 s, karidns
writes a warning in the category \fBgeneral\fR (\fB[GC] the oldest retired zone
snapshot has waited N s ...: held by ...\fR, naming the kind of reader slot or
the retained references), at most once a minute while it waits
.IP \(bu 2
Frontend process health status
.IP \(bu 2
Security counters: RRL dropped/slipped responses, Extended DNS Error counters
(18 Prohibited, 20 Not Authoritative, 21 Not Supported, other) and truncated
dnstap messages
.IP \(bu 2
dnstap (0.7): \fBdnstap dropped (queue full): N\fR (messages lost because a ring
was full: the collector reads too slowly, or \fBqueue\-size\fR is too small),
\fBdnstap dropped (not connected): N\fR (messages lost while no collector was
connected), \fBdnstap connected: yes|no|no (not configured)\fR and \fBdnstap
reconnects: N\fR (connections made by the background retry); see karidns(8)
\fBdnstap\fR "Loss". Without a \fBdnstap\fR block the counters are 0, as
\fBdnstap truncated\fR is
.RE
.TP 8
\fBreload [zone [view] [force]]\fR
Without arguments, re-read the configuration file and \fBall\fR zone files (see
\fBreconfig\fR for the replies; a full reload also drops the changes of dynamic
updates), then, when the configuration has a \fBdatabase\fR block, ask the
database loader to compare its databases with the zones served: the loader sends
the zones that are new, whose serial differs, that the change log
(\fBkaridns_changes\fR, see \fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR) lists as edited, or
whose last version was refused, and a \fBZONE_DELETE\fR for the zones that left
the database. The reply does not wait for the loader: \fBOK reloaded; DB reload
requested (request N)\fR; \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the batch when it is
done. With a zone name, reload only that zone: a primary zone re-reads its zone
file (errors such as a missing file, a parse error, a missing SOA or a running
AXFR are reported), and a secondary zone starts a new transfer from its
primary. For a \fBprogram zone\fR (\fBtype program\fR, 0.6.2) the operator who
names the zone asks for its data to be read again, and the plugin is dealt with
by the first of these that applies: (1) the zone's program settings
(\fBprogram\fR, \fBprogram\-args\fR, \fBprogram\-user\fR,
\fBprogram\-timeout\fR, \fBprogram\-max\-failures\fR,
\fBdisable\-auto\-tc\-flag\fR, \fBprogram\-reload\-line\fR) in the active
configuration are not those of its running plugin: a new plugin is started, and
when it is up the old one is stopped, as for a reload; (2) the settings are the
same and the zone has \fBprogram\-reload\-line yes;\fR: the plugin gets the
\fBRELOAD\fR line and is not restarted; (3) the settings are the same and the
zone has no \fBprogram\-reload\-line\fR (or \fBno\fR): a plugin without that
line can read its data again only by starting again, so a new plugin with the
same settings is started and replaces the old one in the same way (queries are
answered throughout). This differs from a full \fBreload\fR (and \fBreconfig\fR,
\fBSIGHUP\fR), which leaves a plugin whose settings did not change alone, with
the \fBRELOAD\fR line where configured. The reload of a zone does not read
\fBkaridns.conf\fR again (run \fBreconfig\fR or a full \fBreload\fR first to
apply an edit; those already replace the plugins of changed zones, so case 1
arises when that replacement was refused or has not been answered yet, and the
reload asks for it again at once). \fBforce\fR has no effect. The replies: \fBOK
reloaded (program zone: the program settings changed; a new plugin was asked
for, the old one answers until it is up)\fR (1), \fBOK reloaded (program zone:
RELOAD line sent to the plugin)\fR (2), \fBOK reloaded (program zone: no
program\-reload\-line; a new plugin with the same settings was asked for, the
old one answers until it is up)\fR (3), \fBOK reloaded (program zone: a plugin
was asked for; the zone answers when it is up)\fR (a plugin that never came up,
or a refused replacement, is asked for again), \fBOK reloaded (program zone: a
new plugin with these settings is already on its way)\fR, and \fBERROR no plugin
runs for this zone: allow\-program\-zones is not yes, or the zone has no
program\fR. A zone that is not in \fBkaridns.conf\fR is looked up in the
database. For a \fBdatabase zone\fR the loader reads that zone only (also a zone
just added to the database, which the server does not know yet). When several
views take database zones and no view is named, the loader is asked about every
one of them (a mapping in \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR may have moved the zone)
and the reply is one line, \fB<z>: view <v1>: <result>; view <v2>: <result>\fR,
\fBOK\fR when at least one view has the zone and \fBERROR\fR when it is rejected
or failed there or when no view has it (\fBzone not found (it is not in the
database of any view that takes database zones)\fR); a named view gives the
single-view replies below, converts it, and sends it when its content differs
from the version the server serves, whatever its serial; \fBforce\fR sends it
even when the content is the same (D1a: not a zone that is out of memory and
unchanged; there is nothing in memory to replace, the next query reads it from
the database, and the reply is \fBunchanged, serial <n> (not in
memory)\fR). \fBkarictl\fR waits for the loader's answer, but the server does
not: the connection is parked and answered when the result arrives, or after
\fBdb\-reload\-timeout\fR seconds (\fBERROR timeout: ...\fR; the reload may
still be applied). At most 32 clients wait at once; the next ones get \fBERROR
too many reloads are waiting for the database loader; try again
later\fR. Replies (all \fBOK\fR unless marked):
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): applied, serial <n>\fR: the new version is served.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): unchanged, serial <n>\fR: the loader found the
content equal to the served version. With memory-limit, \fB (not in memory)\fR
follows for a zone whose data is out of memory; it is not read, also with
\fBforce\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): loaded, serial <n>, but hidden by the zone in
karidns.conf\fR (or \fB... by a catalog member of the same name\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK zone <z> (view <v>): deleted (it is no longer in the database)\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR zone <z> (view <v>): rejected: <reason>; the last good version, if any,
keeps serving\fR: the server refused the zone.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR zone <z> (view <v>): failed: <reason>\fR: the loader could not read or
convert the zone (a bad row, a missing SOA, an unreadable database); the last
good version keeps serving.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR zone not found (...)\fR: the database does not have the zone (or the
view does not exist).
.IP \(bu 2
several views, none named: \fBOK zone <z>: view a: not in the database; view b:
applied, serial <n>\fR: the result of each view that takes database zones, in
configuration order (\fBnot in the database\fR, \fBapplied, serial <n>\fR,
\fBunchanged, serial <n>\fR, \fBdeleted (...)\fR, \fBrejected: ...\fR,
\fBfailed: ...\fR).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl reload                       # configuration, zone files, and a comparison of the databases
karictl reload example.test          # one zone, from its file or from the database
karictl reload example.test force    # send the database zone even if it is unchanged
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBreconfig\fR
Re-read the configuration file and apply it (added or removed zones, ACLs, rate
limits, ...). Zone files whose modification time and size have not changed are
not re-read. This is the same as sending \fBSIGHUP\fR to the server. When the
\fBzone { }\fR blocks of every view are the ones of the running configuration,
the zones served from the database are taken over as they are (a reload at
1,000,000 database zones takes about 30 ms instead of walking every zone), and
the loader is told about the new configuration afterwards. \fBreload\fR and
\fBreconfig\fR answer when the configuration and the zones are applied, not when
the memory they replaced is freed (0.7): a thread that stays in a read section
of the server only keeps that memory longer (\fBretired zone versions waiting\fR
in \fBstatus\fR). \fBreload\fR (without arguments) and \fBreconfig\fR answer:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOK\fR (\fBOK reloaded\fR for \fBreload\fR): the configuration was applied and
every zone loaded. When a setting that needs a restart changed, \fB(restart
needed for: port, ...)\fR follows.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR configuration applied, but N zone(s) failed to load: <zones>\fR: the
new configuration is in use, and the listed zones (also logged to syslog) answer
SERVFAIL until their files can be loaded. A zone transfer that is being sent
does not make a zone fail (0.7: before, a reload waited 5 s for it and listed
the zone here); it goes on with the version it started with.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR configuration not applied: <reason>\fR: the file could not be read, has
an error (see syslog or \fBkaricheck conf\fR), or a log file could not be
opened; the running configuration is kept.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBERROR reload postponed: ...\fR: a thread of the server was still reading the
configuration before the last one. The configuration is kept in two buffers and
the new one is read into the one that thread may still hold, so the reload is
not done now. \fBreload\fR and \fBreconfig\fR first wait for such readers as
long as they last in normal operation: the longest \fBprogram\-timeout\fR or
\fBforward\-timeout\fR of the configuration plus 0.5 s, at least 1 s and at most
5 s (a worker that waits for a program zone's plugin or a forward zone's
forwarders reads the configuration meanwhile). Only a reader that stays longer
gives this reply, within that time. The server then applies the reload by
itself, with what the command was to do (\fBreload\fR: every zone file and the
request to the database loader), within a second of the reader leaving; the log
says when (\fBThe postponed reload was applied\fR). karictl exits with status 3
for this reply, as for every \fBERROR\fR. A zone transfer that waits for a slow
client is not such a reader (0.7).
.PP
Every \fBERROR\fR reply makes \fBkarictl\fR exit with status 3.
.RE
.TP 8
\fBstop\fR
Stop the \fBkaridns\fR daemon.
.TP 8
\fBnotify <zone> [view]\fR
Send DNS NOTIFY messages for \fB<zone>\fR now: to the \fBalso\-notify\fR servers
and to the addresses of the zone's apex NS hosts, except the host named in the
SOA MNAME field (RFC 1996 \[u00A7]3.2). Zones served from a database are found
too; one that is hidden by a configured zone or a catalog member of the same
name answers \fBERROR zone is not served from the database ...\fR.
.TP 8
\fBretransfer <zone> [view]\fR
For a secondary zone (including a catalog member zone), discard the current
serial and start a new transfer (AXFR) from the primary immediately. For any
other zone the server answers \fBERROR zone is not a secondary\fR and karictl
exits with status 3; the zone is not changed. A \fBSLAVE\fR or \fBCONSUMER\fR
zone from the database (milestone B7b) is a secondary and is transferred again
like any other; a \fBNATIVE\fR / \fBMASTER\fR zone from the database is a
primary zone: the answer is \fBERROR zone is a primary zone from the database;
read it again with 'karictl reload <zone>'\fR.
.TP 8
\fBrollback <zone> [view] [local]\fR
Put the zone back to the version it had before its last reload. The server keeps
the version that a \fBzone-file reload\fR (\fBreload\fR, \fBreload <zone>\fR,
\fBreconfig\fR that re-reads the file) or an \fBinstall from the database\fR
replaces, for \fBrollback\-window\fR (default 1 hour; \fB0\fR keeps nothing;
\fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR, see karidns(8)). A dynamic update, a
zone transfer and a rollback do not replace it: after a reload and three
updates, \fBrollback\fR goes back to the version before the reload and the three
updates are gone with it. A reload that brings the very same content does not
replace it either, so a second \fBreload\fR of the file you just fixed does not
push the good version out. The old content is published again with \fBSOA serial
= the current serial + 1\fR (RFC 1982; a serial of 4294967295 gives 1), an IXFR
difference is recorded and NOTIFY is sent, as for a reload, so the secondaries
follow and never see a serial that goes back. Reply: \fBOK rolled back: serial
<new> (was <current>)\fR. \fBzonestatus\fR shows \fB rolled back (serial <new>,
from <current>)\fR until the next publication (a reload, an update, a database
install), and, while a previous version is kept, \fB previous version kept
(serial <n>, <bytes> bytes, <s> s left)\fR. With \fBlocal\fR the old version is
published \fBunchanged, with its old serial, on this server only\fR: no NOTIFY
is sent and the IXFR history of the zone is dropped, so a secondary that has the
newer serial does not follow (it never accepts a lower serial) and a secondary
that is behind transfers the whole zone. Use it for a signed zone, or to look at
the old data. The view name is written first when there are both: \fBrollback
example.test internal local\fR (a view that is itself named \fBlocal\fR is
written twice: \fBrollback example.test local local\fR). Replies that are
\fBERROR\fR (karictl exits with 3, nothing is changed):
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBno previous version: ...\fR: the zone was not reloaded within
\fBrollback\-window\fR since it was loaded, the window ran out, or it was rolled
back already (\fBonce per reload\fR; there is no redo; reload the file again to
get a version to go back to).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrollback\-window is 0 for this zone: no previous version is kept\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrollback is for primary zones; a secondary gets its data from the primary\fR:
a secondary, a catalog member, a \fBforward\fR zone. A catalog zone (\fBa
catalog zone cannot be rolled back\fR) and a \fBprogram\fR zone are refused too.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBzone is signed; roll back in the DB and re\-sign\fR: the version to go back
to holds RRSIG or DNSKEY records. KariDNS cannot sign, and a new serial would
leave the SOA with a signature that does not match. Roll back where the zone is
signed, or use \fBlocal\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthe zone was taken out of memory; nothing to roll back\fR (a database zone
whose data left memory, or one hidden by a zone of the same name). What a later
reload does:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone file.\fR \fBreload <zone>\fR and a full \fBreload\fR keep the
rolled-back version while the file is the one that was read (same modification
time and size), and say so (\fBOK kept the rolled\-back version: ...\fR). Fix
the file and reload, or \fBreload <zone> force\fR to read it again (which brings
the data back that you rolled back). A change only in a file that the zone
\fB$INCLUDE\fRs is not noticed by that check: use \fBforce\fR. \fBreconfig\fR
already skips files that did not change. A restart reads the file again.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDatabase zone.\fR The server compares the loader's (source id, serial,
digest) with what it recorded when it installed the zone, and a rollback does
not change that record, so the rolled-back version stays until the data in the
database change; the next change is installed normally. Meanwhile the zone is
pinned in memory like one that a dynamic update changed (\fBdbstatus <zone>\fR
says \fBrolled back: serial N, from M; pinned in memory ...\fR), because the
database cannot give the old version back. With several servers on one database,
a rollback on one makes them differ until the database changes; a rollback in
the database (\fBkaridns_*\fR data) is the durable fix.
.IP \(bu 2
The command needs the permission \fBrollback\fR of a restricted control key
(\fBallow\-commands { rollback; }\fR), and is logged with the key name like the
other commands.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl reload example.test          # the new file has a mistake
karictl rollback example.test        # serial N+1, the old data, the secondaries follow
karictl rollback example.test local  # the old version as it was, this server only
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBzonestatus <zone> [view]\fR
Display the SOA serial and refresh interval of \fB<zone>\fR (\fBOK serial=<n>
refresh=<n> retry=<n> expire=<n>\fR; for a secondary the values the zone uses,
held to \fBmin\-refresh\-time\fR ... \fBmax\-retry\-time\fR). For a zone from
the database the line goes on with \fBsource=database view=<v> id=<domains.id>
result=<applied|rejected|shadowed (hidden by ...)>\fR and the last error, if
any. A secondary that applies settings received from its primary (Extended AXFR
version 3, see karidns(8)) adds \fBsettings=primary\fR (and \fBnote: ...\fR when
part of them is not in force, e.g. a TSIG key the received \fBallow\-transfer\fR
names is not defined here).
.TP 8
\fBobservatory [zone [view]]\fR
Display per-zone statistics for all zones, or only for the given zone (and
view): role (primary/secondary), SOA serial, query counts (total and TCP),
response codes (NOERROR, NXDOMAIN, NODATA, SERVFAIL, REFUSED), EDNS/DO/ECS query
counts, RRL dropped/slipped counts, successful inbound AXFR/IXFR transfers
(secondary, with \fBFailed\-refreshes\fR: the refreshes that failed at every
primary asked) or NOTIFY messages sent (primary) with the time of the last one,
and response cache hits and misses (the cache is shared by all zones, so it has
no per-zone size). A zone that other views share with \fBzone { in\-view }\fR
(karidns(8) "Zones shared between views") is listed once, in the view that owns
it, as \fBZone: <name> (view: <owner>, shared to views: <a>, <b>)\fR;
\fBstatus\fR counts it once, and \fBnotify\fR, \fBzonestatus\fR and \fBreload\fR
name it with or without a view (a referencing view's name reaches the owner's
zone).
.TP 8
\fBstats [\-f prometheus|json] [zone [view]] [\-\-zones PATTERN] [\-\-limit N] [\-\-after CURSOR]\fR
Print the counters of the server, its views and zones, for monitoring. The
default format is the Prometheus text exposition format (metric names
\fBkaridns_*\fR, labels \fBzone\fR, \fBview\fR, \fBrcode\fR, \fBproto\fR, ...),
which the node_exporter textfile collector reads; \fB\-f json\fR (or
\fB\-\-format json\fR) prints one JSON object. (\fB\-f\fR after \fBstats\fR is
the format; the \fB\-f\fR before the command is still the configuration
file.) The exit status is 0 only when the whole output was written, so
\fBkarictl stats > file.$$ && mv file.$$ file\fR never leaves a half-written
file. The output has a \fBserver\fR part (version, boot time, zone count,
workers, TCP clients, zone memory, query, response, EDNS, NOTIFY, transfer,
response-cache, rate-limit and Extended DNS Error counters, failed transfers,
failed reloads, raised alerts, the reclaim queue (the gauges
\fBkaridns_reclaim_snapshots_waiting\fR,
\fBkaridns_reclaim_oldest_snapshot_age_seconds\fR,
\fBkaridns_reclaim_zone_versions_waiting\fR and the counter
\fBkaridns_reclaim_warnings_total\fR; in JSON \fBreclaim\fR
\fB{snapshots_waiting, oldest_snapshot_age_ms, zone_versions_waiting,
warnings}\fR in \fBserver\fR) and dnstap (\fBkaridns_dnstap_truncated_total\fR,
\fBkaridns_dnstap_dropped_total{reason="queue_full"|"not_connected"}\fR, the
gauge \fBkaridns_dnstap_connected\fR (1 or 0; 0 also without a \fBdnstap\fR
block) and \fBkaridns_dnstap_reconnects_total\fR; in JSON
\fBdnstap_truncated\fR, \fBdnstap_dropped\fR \fB{queue_full, not_connected}\fR,
\fBdnstap_connected\fR (true / false) and \fBdnstap_reconnects\fR)), then
\fBviews\fR (zones, queries and responses summed per view; only when every zone
is listed), then one set of series \fBper zone\fR (queries by transport,
responses by RCODE, EDNS / DO / ECS queries, rate-limit drops and slips, NOTIFY,
transfers in and their failures, response cache hits and misses, SOA serial,
role, time of the last transfer). The counters are cumulative since the server
started (Prometheus \fBcounter\fRs); a zone that a reload replaces starts again
at 0, which \fBrate()\fR handles as a counter reset. Queries that no zone
answered (REFUSED for a name outside every zone, FORMERR, NOTIMP, ...) belong to
no zone: they are in \fBkaridns_unzoned_responses_total{rcode}\fR, and
\fBkaridns_responses_total{rcode}\fR is the zones' responses plus
those. \fBkaridns_queries_total{proto}\fR counts the queries answered for a
zone. \fBstats <zone> [view]\fR prints one zone (the same as \fBobservatory
<zone>\fR, without the other zones). Without a zone name every zone is listed,
fetched in pages of 2000 (the server never builds one reply with all zones) in
the order of its zone tables (the order of the names' hashes, not alphabetical;
a zone added or removed while the listing runs may or may not be
seen). \fB\-\-zones PATTERN\fR lists only the zones whose name matches the glob
(\fB*\fR, \fB?\fR, \fB[a\-z]\fR, at most four \fB*\fR; case-insensitive;
\fBexample.*\fR matches \fBexample.test.\fR), \fB\-\-limit N\fR stops after N
zones and, if there are more, ends the output with \fB# more zones: karictl
stats \-\-after <cursor>\fR (JSON: \fB"more":true,"after":"<cursor>"\fR), and
\fB\-\-after CURSOR\fR continues there. A cursor is a position in the server's
zone tables, so a page costs the server the page, not the number of zones (at
most 65536 table slots are looked at for one page; a glob that matches little
gives short pages that karictl asks for one after the other). A cursor from
before a reload that rebuilt the tables is refused (\fBcursor is stale\fR, exit
3); a text listing that meets this while it runs starts again (up to three
times), a JSON listing, which is written as it comes, stops with the error. The
totals of the zones' counters in the server part (queries by transport, NODATA,
EDNS, NOTIFY, transfers) are summed with one walk of all zones per command (see
the release notes for the time at 1,000,000 zones). Values with white space are
refused (write a space in a name as \fB\e032\fR). The text format is valid
exposition format whatever the number of zones: karictl collects the zones first
(184 bytes and the zone name, about 25 bytes, per zone: about 210 MB for
1,000,000 zones; use \fB\-\-zones\fR and \fB\-\-limit\fR to bound it) and writes
every metric family once, in one piece, with \fB# HELP\fR and \fB# TYPE\fR
once. \fB\-f json\fR is written as the pages come and keeps nothing. The command
needs the read permission of a restricted control key, like \fBobservatory\fR.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl stats > /var/tmp/node_exporter/karidns.prom.$$ && mv /var/tmp/node_exporter/karidns.prom.$$ /var/tmp/node_exporter/karidns.prom
karictl stats \-f json example.test
karictl stats \-\-zones '*.example.test' \-\-limit 100
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBdbstatus [zone [view]]\fR
Show the state of the zones served from a database (\fBoptions { database
{ ... }; }\fR, SQLite3 or PostgreSQL through \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR). Without
arguments: whether the loader is connected (with its process ID, version and
drivers, and since when), how many DB zones the server holds (served, hidden by
a zone of the same name in \fBkaridns.conf\fR, hidden by a catalog member of the
same name, and zones with a rejected version), the last batch the loader
delivered (id, kind \fBstartup\fR / \fBfull\fR / \fBdiff\fR / \fBzone\fR /
\fBauto\fR; a \fBreload\fR is a \fBdiff\fR batch, a \fBreload <zone>\fR a
\fBzone\fR batch, time, and its applied / rejected / shadowed / deleted counts),
the state of auto reload (\fBauto reload: off\fR, or \fBon\fR with
poll-interval, coalesce, the number of auto batches since the start and, per
view, the last poll, the applied change log position, the pending changes and
the last error; B9; under a view a line \fBwaiting for the signer: N zone(s): a,
b, ...\fR when zones with the metadata \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = \fB1\fR
changed and wait for \fBkaridns\-signer\fR to sign them and ask for a reload,
see \fBsigner\-wait\fR in \fBkaridns(8)\fR), for a PostgreSQL view whose schema
has the multi-server tables (B11) one line \fBreload requests (view <name>):
server\-id <id>, last applied generation <n>, ok yes|no, status written
<time>|never, last error: <text>|none\fR (the id this server reports under in
\fBkaridns_server_status\fR, the highest \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR
generation it has applied, whether every zone of that reload loaded, when its
status row was last written, and why a read of the requests or a write of the
status row failed; the server keeps serving when it cannot write the row), the
size of the zone versions and IXFR deltas (the same numbers as in \fBstatus\fR),
the fetches of whole zones from the database (\fBfetch: rate=N/s
refused_by_rate=N issued=N\fR; \fBissued\fR counts the zones the Backend asked
the loader for: a query, a transfer, the warm-up), then \fBfetchq: in_flight=N
places=N peak=N table_full=N ring_dropped=N park_full=N park_timeout=N
loader_busy=N ingest_busy=P% hist=A,B,C,D,E\fR (0.7: where a fetch is held up;
all counted since the start except \fBin_flight\fR and \fBingest_busy\fR:
\fBin_flight\fR and \fBpeak\fR are the fetches on their way now and the most
there have been at one time; \fBplaces\fR, the places of
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR in use now (whole-zone fetches queued for the ingest
thread or in flight: 0 at rest); \fBtable_full\fR, requests refused because the
256 places of the fetch table were all taken; \fBring_dropped\fR, requests the
workers could not hand to the ingest thread because their ring of 128 was full;
\fBpark_full\fR, queries that found the table of parked queries full
(\fBpark\-max\fR); \fBpark_timeout\fR, parked queries whose zone was not there
before \fBpark\-timeout\fR; \fBloader_busy\fR, fetches the loader turned away
because its queue was full; \fBingest_busy\fR, the share of the last ten seconds
the ingest thread was working instead of waiting, 0 for the first ten seconds;
\fBhist\fR, the time from the request to the install of the fetches that
succeeded: up to 1 ms, up to 10 ms, up to 100 ms, up to 1 s, more; see
"\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows it" under "Memory limit (D1a)" in
\fBkaridns(8)\fR), the NOTIFY of zones that are out of memory (D1a: \fBnotify of
zones out of memory: queued=N deferred=N\fR, the zones whose serial changed
while their data was out of memory that were handed to the control thread, and
the ones that did not fit in the ring of 1024 and are queued later), the zone
transfers of zones that are out of memory (D1a, \fBtransfers of zones out of
memory: waited=N loaded=N failed=N summary_answers=N marked=N timeout=Nms\fR:
transfers that had to wait for the zone, the zones they got, the ones that ended
with SERVFAIL, the IXFRs answered from the summary without reading the zone, the
zones marked evict-next, and \fBtransfer\-fetch\-timeout\fR) and the cost of the
zone directory (\fBzone directory: N elements, B bytes in the registry + I bytes
in the indexes of the views (P bytes per zone)\fR; the entries and images are
not in it), then \fBalias: scans=N\fR (0.7: how many times the thread that
resolves the targets of ALIAS records scanned the zone directory for them since
the start; a scan follows a zone with ALIAS records coming, changing, going or
being taken out of memory and a reconfiguration, not every zone that is fetched
or evicted, so the number stays where it is while zones without ALIAS records
come and go; see "Expansion" under POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS in
\fBkaridns(8)\fR). The warm-up and the cache (D1a step 7): \fBresidency:
directory=N resident=N out_of_memory=N evictions=N\fR (the zones of the
directory with and without data in memory), \fBqueries: answered=N parked=N
hit_rate=P%\fR (the queries the zones answered, and the ones that had to wait
for their zone; the hit rate is the share answered by a zone already in memory),
\fBwarm: received=0|1 queued=N loaded=N skipped=N (memory limit N, already in
memory or gone N) failed=N pending=N in_flight=N\fR (the zones of the loader's
hot list: queued to read, read, not read because the memory limit's high water
mark was reached or because they were in memory or gone, failed, still waiting,
being read), \fBwarm\-before\-serve: off\fR, \fBholding the queries (N ms
left)\fR or \fBqueries accepted after N ms\fR (the queries are held while the
hot list is read; the control channel is not), and \fBstate\-file: <path>
at_start=... hot_lists_sent=N ... acks: written=N unchanged=N failed=N ...\fR
(what the loader found in its state file at its start, how many hot lists the
Backend sent, and what the loader answered; \fBstate\-file: none\fR without
\fBloader\-state\-file\fR). These lines come after the \fBzone directory:\fR
line; the lines before keep their form. Then, the totals since the server
started, and the last protocol error of the loader link. With a zone name (and
optionally a view): that zone's view, database id, SOA serial, status
(\fBapplied\fR, \fBrejected\fR or \fBshadowed\fR) and the last error, then, for
a \fBSLAVE\fR or \fBCONSUMER\fR zone (B7b), its type, masters and TSIG key and
the last successful transfer with the serial (the data of such a zone is kept in
memory only; \fBsettings from the primary: in force\fR when the primary sent
settings, B8t-2), then (B7a) the settings it runs with: where they come from
(\fBprofile 'name'\fR with the zone's own metadata overriding it, \fBthe zone's
own metadata\fR, or \fBnone\fR), its \fBallow\-transfer\fR and
\fBallow\-update\fR entries with their match mode, the entries
\fBacl\-ceiling\fR cuts away (\fBlies outside acl\-ceiling\fR, \fBis narrowed by
acl\-ceiling\fR, or \fBno acl\-ceiling is configured: these ACLs refuse
everybody\fR) and a \fBsettings error\fR line when a TSIG key the settings name
is no longer defined in \fBkaridns.conf\fR. The summary also counts the distinct
settings (zones with the same settings share one block) and the zones that name
an undefined key. A zone that was rejected keeps serving its last good version
(its serial is shown). Without a \fBdatabase\fR block the server answers
\fBERROR no database block is configured\fR.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl dbstatus
karictl dbstatus example.test
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBtrace [level]\fR
Change the debug level in force of the server (0.7; \fBrndc trace\fR): without
an argument one more (at most 99), with one that level, a number from 0 to
99. The reply is \fBOK debug level is now N\fR; a level that is not a number
from 0 to 99 is answered \fBERROR trace: the debug level must be a number from 0
to 99\fR, a second argument \fBERROR trace takes one argument at most: ...\fR
(exit status 3, the level is not changed). The server logs each change as
\fB[Control] debug level is now N\fR (category \fBgeneral\fR, notice). The
channels with \fBseverity dynamic\fR write the messages up to the level, and the
built-in \fBdefault_debug\fR (the file \fBkaridns.run\fR in \fBoptions {
directory }\fR) writes everything up to it while it is above 0; see karidns(8),
\fBlogging\fR. The level is not kept across a restart (\fBkaridns \-d\fR sets it
at the start). Every process of the server follows (0.7): the sandboxed process,
which has the control channel, hands the level to the supervisor (with the
supervisors of the plugins and of \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR), the UDP routers, the
connect broker and the loader, and a loader that is started again later comes up
with the level in force.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl trace 3      # the debug 3 lines of query\-errors (why a query was REFUSED) appear in the dynamic channels
karictl notrace
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fBnotrace\fR
Set the debug level to 0 (\fBrndc notrace\fR); the reply is \fBOK debug level is
now 0\fR. An argument is refused (\fBERROR notrace takes no argument\fR).
.RS 8
.PP
\fBtrace\fR and \fBnotrace\fR need the permission \fBtrace\fR of a restricted
control key (\fBallow\-commands { trace; }\fR); a \fBread\-only yes\fR key alone
does not have it.
.RE
.TP 8
\fBtsig\-keygen [keyname]\fR
Generate a 256-bit random TSIG secret with OpenSSL \fBRAND_bytes(3)\fR and print
a \fBkey\fR block (\fBalgorithm hmac\-sha256\fR) ready for
\fBkaridns.conf\fR. The default key name is \fBtransfer\-key\fR. This command
does not contact the server and does not read the configuration file.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karictl tsig\-keygen transfer\-key
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE (karictl.conf)
By default, \fBkarictl\fR reads the shared secret from
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karictl.conf\fR. The package installs
\fBkarictl.conf.sample\fR next to it.
.SS Configuration Syntax
.RS 2
.nf
socket "/var/run/karidns/control.sock";
\&
key "karictl" {
    algorithm "hmac\-sha256";
    secret "UkVQTEFDRS1NRTpvcGVuc3NsLXJhbmQtYmFzZTY0LTMy"; // placeholder: replace with `openssl rand \-base64 32`
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
The \fBsecret\fR defined in \fBkarictl.conf\fR must match the secret configured
in the \fBcontrol\-channel\fR block of \fBkaridns.conf\fR.
.PP
The file is not parsed as a full configuration file: \fBkarictl\fR takes the
quoted value that follows the \fBfirst\fR occurrence of the word \fBsecret\fR,
and the quoted value that follows the first occurrence of the word \fBsocket\fR,
anywhere in the file \fBoutside comments (\fB#\fB, \fB//\fB, \fB/* */\fB) and
quoted strings\fR (the sample's own comment mentions \fB{ secret "..."; }\fR,
which an earlier version took for the secret). White space after the \fB=\fR
padding of the secret is ignored. The value after the first \fBalgorithm\fR
(quoted or not) selects the HMAC: \fBhmac\-md5\fR, \fBhmac\-sha1\fR,
\fBhmac\-sha224\fR, \fBhmac\-sha256\fR (default), \fBhmac\-sha384\fR or
\fBhmac\-sha512\fR; it must match the server's \fBcontrol\-channel { algorithm
}\fR, and an unsupported name exits with status 2. The key name is not used. The
secret may decode to at most 256 bytes. \fBkarictl\fR prints a warning when the
file is readable by group or other users; it should have mode \fB0600\fR.
.PP
\fBRestricted keys.\fR The server can hold several keys, each allowed to run
only some commands (\fBcontrol\-channel { keys { } }\fR in \fBkaridns.conf\fR,
"Restricted control keys"). \fBkarictl\fR needs nothing for that: give each user
a \fBkarictl.conf\fR with the \fBsecret\fR and the \fBalgorithm\fR of the key it
should use. The server finds the key by the response to its challenge. A command
the key may not run is answered with \fBERROR command not allowed for key
"<name>"\fR and \fBkarictl\fR exits with status 3; the server logs it with the
key name. To let a user other than the server's in, the server's
\fBcontrol\-channel { allow\-group }\fR must name a group that user belongs to
(the socket is then group-writable); the user needs the \fBkarictl.conf\fR of
its key and no access to \fBkaridns.conf\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
Command executed successfully (including \fBtsig\-keygen\fR and \fB\-v\fR).
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
Socket path too long, connection failure to the control socket, or failure to
receive the challenge.
.TP 8
\fB2\fR
Usage error (no command, an unknown option, \fBnotify\fR / \fBretransfer\fR /
\fBzonestatus\fR / \fBrollback\fR without a zone name; the usage text goes to
standard error and nothing is sent to the server), or the secret could not be
read or decoded from the configuration file, the challenge was invalid, or
authentication failed. A command the server does not know is not a usage error
for \fBkarictl\fR: the server answers \fBERROR unknown command\fR and the exit
status is 3. \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-\-help\fR print the usage text to standard
output and exit with 0.
.TP 8
\fB3\fR
The server returned an error (\fBERROR ...\fR, including a failed \fBreload\fR
or \fBreconfig\fR, a \fBtrace\fR level that is not a number from 0 to 99, a
command that the key is not allowed to run, a database zone that was rejected or
could not be read, and a reload that timed out) or an invalid \fBstatus\fR or
\fBobservatory\fR response (also \fBdbstatus\fR without a database block).
.SH FILES
.TP 8
\fB/usr/local/etc/karidns/karictl.conf\fR
Default control channel client configuration file.
.TP 8
\fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR
Default UNIX domain socket endpoint monitored by the \fBkaridns\fR daemon.
